nmos-dcm (2.2.9+mxl1-9) unstable; urgency=low

  * staged_patch() and _onReceiverActivated() now log every step of a
    receiver PATCH (the incoming request, why an activation was a no-op
    - no static topology, unreadable transport_params, nothing to
      reverse on a null-clear - and the mxl_domain_id/mxl_flow_id it
      actually saw), instead of several of those paths silently
      returning with no trace at all.
  * A direct receiver PATCH from one real flow straight to another (no
    null step in between) no longer leaves the old flow's
    mxl-fabrics-proxy subscription entry and its symlink under the
    input's flow config dir behind indefinitely - _onReceiverActivated()
    now hands the previously-synced (endpoint, domain_id, flow_id) to
    the new _switchReceiver(), which unsyncs it before syncing the new
    one whenever the two differ.
  * _detectFlowsUnderInput() now picks the most-recently-linked flow of
    each essence type (by symlink mtime) instead of whichever flow uuid
    happens to sort first alphabetically - the previous ordering meant a
    stale symlink (e.g. one surviving an nmos-dcm restart, since
    _receiverActiveFlow's own bookkeeping is in-memory only) could keep
    winning over whichever flow was actually just patched, regardless of
    _switchReceiver() above.
  * Package (and its systemd unit) renamed from
    nmos-device-connection-management to nmos-dcm - shorter to type in
    systemctl/journalctl commands day to day. The vendored upstream repo
    directory itself (thirdparty/nmos/nmos-device-connection-management-ri/)
    keeps its original name - this rename is packaging-only.
  * DC_MXL_SENDERS_CONFIG/DC_MXL_RECEIVERS_CONFIG's own defaults move from
    /etc/nmos-connection/ to /etc/nmos-dcm/, matching the package rename
    above - senders.json/receivers.json already provisioned under the old
    path on a deployed box need to be moved there by hand before
    restarting on the new version, nothing does that automatically.
  * A PATCH clearing a receiver's mxl_domain_id/mxl_flow_id back to null
    (removing a stream) is now handled as the reverse of the activation
    sync: _onReceiverActivated() remembers the (endpoint, domain_id,
    flow_id) each real activation last wired up, and a null-activation
    unwinds it via the new _unsyncReceiver() - removes this flow's id
    from its mxl-fabrics-proxy subscription entry (dropping the entry
    entirely once none are left), reloads the proxy, unlinks this flow's
    symlink from the input's flow config dir, and regenerates config.json
    from whatever flows remain (or removes config.json entirely if no
    video flow is left, matching mxl-dcstranscode-input.sh's own contract
    of exiting when it's missing), then restarts dc-input. A redundant
    null-activation (no prior synced flow to reverse) is a safe no-op,
    same as before this change. The domain directory itself is never
    removed - other flows may still be using it.
  * _generateInputConfig() now polls (up to 5s) for this receiver's own
    <mxl_domain_id>/<flow_id>.mxl-flow/flow_def.json to actually appear
    before scanning for it - mxl-fabrics-proxy's /v1/reload response only
    means it re-read its subscription config, not that it has already
    established the subscription and pulled real grains from the remote
    source, so the flow directory materializes asynchronously a short
    while after reload() returns. Without this, input config regeneration
    would spuriously fail with "no video flow found" on a race, even
    though the flow shows up correctly moments later.
  * mxl-fabrics-proxy's real config file path is now discovered directly
    from the running container (`docker inspect <container>`, matching
    its own --config <container-path> arg against the bind mount that
    covers it) instead of assuming a fixed default - stays correct
    without an env var needing to be kept in sync with however the
    container actually happens to be started. DC_MXL_FABRICS_PROXY_CONFIG
    is still an explicit override (checked first); DC_MXL_FABRICS_PROXY_CONTAINER
    overrides the container name to inspect (default: mxl-fabrics-proxy).
    Falls back to the old DEFAULT_FABRICS_PROXY_CONFIG path, with a
    logged warning, if discovery fails for any reason (docker missing,
    container not running, unexpected inspect output).
  * _onReceiverActivated() now only considers a receiver's own endpoints
    entries whose "protocol" is "mxl" (the first one found), instead of
    blindly using endpoints[0] - a receiver whose endpoints list has no
    "mxl" entry at all is warned and skipped, same as an empty list
    already was.
  * _syncReceiver() now creates this receiver's own mxl_domain_id
    directory (mkdir -p, same as make-mxl-config.py's own
    ensure_domain_exists()) before updating/reloading mxl-fabrics-proxy's
    config - a brand new domain that's never been used before would
    otherwise get referenced in domains:/subscriptions: before the
    directory backing it actually exists.

  * MXL transport_params redesigned to match the real AMWA BCP-007-03
    ("NMOS With MXL") spec instead of an invented shape: the previous
    per-leg source_ip/source_port/domain/flow_id/provider/mxl_enabled
    fields are replaced with the spec's own mxl_domain_id/mxl_flow_id,
    with one deliberate deviation - mxl_domain_id accepts any string
    (not just a UUID resolved via a domain_def.json), so the literal
    mxl-fabrics-proxy domain path (e.g. /dev/shm/mxl) can be used
    directly. mxl_flow_id stays spec-strict (uuid, plus "auto" for
    senders only).
  * nmosDriver.py's addMxlSenderToIS05 no longer registers local
    interfaces (getAvailableInterfaces() removed, along with its only
    caller) - there is no source_ip field left for it to populate.
  * DcMxlSender (added in -5) is removed - not needed for now.
    addMxlSenderToIS05 goes back to plain MxlSender, so /active no
    longer carries an ext_mxl_enabled field at all.
  * Real MXL sender/receiver driver logic (static-config bootstrap,
    on-demand POST /api/senders/ and /api/receivers/ creation) is
    extracted out of nmosDriver.py's demo/mock RTP scaffolding into its
    own DcMxlDriver class (nmosconnection/dcMxlDriver.py) - makes it
    obvious which part of this codebase is real DreamCatcher logic vs
    the BBC reference implementation's example driver. NmosDriverWebApi
    now holds a DcMxlDriver instance instead of the mxl methods being
    defined directly on it. activationController (shared by both the
    RTP and MXL paths) moves to its own module
    (nmosconnection/activationController.py) to avoid a circular import
    between nmosDriver.py and dcMxlDriver.py.
  * Fix addReceiverToIS05() calling activateStaged() before
    facadeWrapper.registerReceiver() - activateStaged() fires the
    activation callback synchronously on this first activation, which
    (via activationController.activateReceiver()) calls
    facadeWrapper.updateReceiver(), a KeyError unless
    facadeWrapper.receivers[receiverId] already exists - which
    registerReceiver() is what creates. Broke every mxl receiver's very
    first activation (both the on-demand POST /api/receivers/ path and
    the static-config bootstrap path) ever since the DcMxlDriver
    extraction above reordered setActivateCallback()/activateStaged()
    relative to the original nmosDriver.py code (which happened to avoid
    this by accident - it called activateStaged() before
    setActivateCallback(), so the callback was never actually wired up
    yet the first time). registerReceiver() now runs before
    activateStaged().
  * DcMxlDriver now logs "DreamCatcher MXL driver running" on startup
    (distinct from nmosDriver.py's pre-existing "Mock driver interface
    running", which is just the demo admin API/HttpServer, not proof the
    real mxl driver initialized), plus how many static senders/receivers
    it actually bootstrapped from DC_MXL_SENDERS_CONFIG/
    DC_MXL_RECEIVERS_CONFIG.
  * Receivers get a fixed-id static bootstrap too:
    DcMxlDriver.bootstrapStaticReceivers(), reading DC_MXL_RECEIVERS_CONFIG
    at startup. Unlike senders, this file carries no mxl_domain_id/
    mxl_flow_id at all - just topology (a JSON object mapping receiver
    uuid -> {"input", "leg", "endpoints": [{"host","port","protocol"}]}):
    which local dcstranscode input a receiver feeds, and which remote
    NMOS endpoint(s) it might pull from. A receiver's actual
    mxl_domain_id/mxl_flow_id only ever arrive later via a real PATCH,
    same as any other receiver - hand-provisioned, not generated (no
    make-mxl-config.py flag for it, unlike senders). The systemd unit now
    also sets DC_MXL_RECEIVERS_CONFIG (default
    /etc/nmos-connection/receivers.json).
  * New: every successful MXL receiver activation with a real (non-null)
    mxl_domain_id/mxl_flow_id - the initial bootstrap if pre-staged, or
    (more commonly) any later PATCH .../receivers/<id>/staged - now
    automatically, in a background thread so the PATCH response is never
    delayed: (1) upserts that flow id into the matching mxl-fabrics-proxy
    subscription entry (matched by source host, additive - doesn't touch
    other sources' entries) in DC_MXL_FABRICS_PROXY_CONFIG (default
    /opt/dreamcatcher/share/mxl/mxl-fabrics-proxy-config.yaml) - the
    domains:/subscriptions: key used is derived from this receiver's own
    mxl_domain_id (its basename, e.g. /dev/shm/mxl -> "mxl"), and both
    the domains: entry and the subscriptions: entry for that key are
    created if missing, without disturbing any other domain/source
    already in the file, (2) POSTs DC_MXL_FABRICS_PROXY_ADMIN_URL +
    /v1/reload (default http://localhost:2283), (3) regenerates this
    receiver's input's own /etc/mxl/flows/input/<input>/config.json:
    symlinks this activation's own flow into
    /etc/mxl/flows/input/<input>/<mxl_flow_id> ->
    <mxl_domain_id>/<mxl_flow_id>.mxl-flow/flow_def.json (same convention
    make-mxl-config.py's own create_flow_symlinks() uses - accumulates
    across separate video/audio/data receiver activations sharing the
    same input, rather than being overwritten each time), then scans that
    input dir's own flow-uuid symlinks (so a flow another receiver
    already linked in stays included, config.json itself skipped by
    name), classifies each by its own "format" field, and calls
    /opt/dreamcatcher/sbin/make-mxl-config.py (full path - unlike
    dc-input, which installs to /usr/sbin/ and is on a systemd service's
    default PATH, make-mxl-config.py installs to /opt/dreamcatcher/sbin/,
    which isn't) with the detected -v/-a/-d files and just the domain (no
    mxl-flow-config-path argument -
    make-mxl-config.py would unlink() each symlink just given to it
    before resolve()-ing it to find its own target, breaking the very
    path it was asked to read); the printed JSON is written to that
    input's own config.json directly, and (4) runs
    `dc-input restart <input>` for this receiver's configured input, so
    it starts from that freshly regenerated config.
    Each step is independent and best-effort - failures are logged, never
    block/fail the activation. New dependency: PyYAML (added to
    install.sh's venv setup and setup.py's install_requires).
  * dcstranscode/scripts/make-mxl-config.py's --nmos-receivers-config is
    removed - it was flow-descriptor-driven, which no longer matches
    DC_MXL_RECEIVERS_CONFIG's new topology-only shape at all.
  * dcstranscode/scripts/make-mxl-config.py's --nmos-senders-config now
    emits mxl_domain_id/mxl_flow_id; the --source-ip/--source-port flags
    added last revision are removed as obsolete under the new schema.
  * DC_MXL_SENDERS_CONFIG's file format changes from a JSON array of
    {"id", "legs", "transport_params"} entries to a JSON object mapping
    sender uuid -> {"legs", "transport_params"} - make-mxl-config.py now
    generates a real random sender id per flow instead of reusing the
    flow's own id as the sender id, and keeps that sender id stable
    across re-runs (looked up by the flow id already present in an
    existing entry's transport_params) instead of only across service
    restarts.

 -- Evertz DreamCatcher <ichvets@evertz.com>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

nmos-dcm (2.2.9+mxl1-8) unstable; urgency=low

  * getAvailableInterfaces() now excludes loopback ("lo") - it was being
    registered first (netifaces.interfaces() lists it first on Linux),
    so defaultSourceSelector() picked 127.0.0.1 as source_ip's "auto"
    resolution instead of a real, reachable data interface.

